<header class="skibby-hero"><p class="section-label">Yorkshire · 17 September 1967</p><h1>Beryl Burton, twelve hours<br/>and a <em>liquorice allsort</em></h1><p>Mike McNamara was on course for the British men’s record. Then Beryl Burton passed him and offered him a sweet.</p></header>
<figure class="skibby-image"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/beryl-burton-1967/beryl-burton-time-trial-wetherby.jpg`} alt="Beryl Burton being held at the start of a time trial in Wetherby"/><figcaption>Beryl Burton at the start of a time trial on Deighton Road in Wetherby. Photo: Brian Townsley · CC BY 2.0 via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="skibby-opening"><p class="section-label">After about 235 miles</p><p>Burton had caught the fastest man in the event. In her later account, she reached for a liquorice allsort, asked whether he wanted it and rode on.</p></section>
<section class="skibby-story"><aside><strong>12</strong><span>hours on<br/>Yorkshire roads</span></aside><div>
<h2>A race against the clock</h2><p>The British 12-hour time trial was simple in form and unforgiving in execution: ride as far as possible in twelve hours. Riders started individually, and their distance was measured when time ran out. There was no mass start, no stages and no mountain to provide a natural climax. Only speed, endurance and the same roads, hour after hour.</p>
<p>On 17 September 1967, Otley Cycle Club held its 12-hour event on Yorkshire roads. Among the entrants was Beryl Burton of Morley Cycling Club. She was 30 and already one of the strongest riders of her generation, with world titles on both road and track.</p>
<p>The women started after the men. Burton could therefore catch riders who had begun ahead of her. One of them was Mike McNamara. He was not merely another competitor on the course. He was riding towards a record of his own.</p>
<h2>The man with a record in reach</h2><p>McNamara was on his way to 276.52 miles, a new British men’s record. Burton caught him after about 235 miles. Her autobiography and a later account by her daughter, Denise Burton-Cole, preserve the small scene that followed: Burton found a liquorice allsort and offered it to him. He accepted. She continued.</p>
<blockquote>It was not a grand gesture. That is why it fits the ride so well: In the middle of a record attempt, there was still room to share what she had in her pocket.</blockquote>
<p>The anecdote has been retold with small variations. Its core, however, comes from the Burton family and McNamara’s own confirmation: She caught him, offered him the sweet and covered the greatest distance that day.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="skibby-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="skibby-context"><div><p class="section-label">When time ran out</p><h2>277.25 miles</h2></div><div><p>Burton’s final distance was 277.25 miles, about 446.2 kilometres. That is an average of roughly 23.1 mph, or 37.2 km/h, for twelve continuous hours. She did more than improve the women’s record. She rode 0.73 miles farther than McNamara’s new British men’s record.</p><p>The result was not produced in a separate women’s race on a shorter course. Burton and McNamara rode the same roads in the same event. When the day ended, she had travelled farther.</p><p>It took two years for a man to ride farther in a British 12-hour event. Burton’s women’s record lasted much longer and was not surpassed for half a century.</p></div></section>
<figure class="skibby-image"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/beryl-burton-1967/beryl-burton-world-championship-1967.jpg`} alt="Beryl Burton at the world championships in Heerlen in September 1967"/><figcaption>Beryl Burton at the world championships in Heerlen on 2 September 1967, two weeks before the 12-hour event. Photo: Jack de Nijs / Anefo / Nationaal Archief · CC BY-SA 3.0 NL via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="skibby-return"><p class="section-label">An unusual measure</p><blockquote>She was not compared with the men to make a headline. The distance made the comparison unavoidable.</blockquote><div><p>Burton won seven world championships and dominated British women’s cycling for decades. The 12-hour ride nevertheless remains an unusually clear expression of her level. In most events, women’s and men’s results were separated by classes, distances and starting fields. Here, the clock and the road provided a common measure.</p><p>The record also says something about the sport of the period. Women had few opportunities for professional support, and women’s road cycling did not enter the Olympic programme until 1984. Burton combined training and competition with work and family life, yet performed at a level the established sport struggled to accommodate.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-finish"><div><p class="section-label">What remained</p><h2>The record and the sweet</h2></div><div><p>The numbers are exact: 277.25 miles against 276.52. They explain what Burton did. The liquorice allsort does not explain the result, but it makes the final hours human.</p><p>Mike McNamara earned his men’s record. Beryl Burton rode farther. And somewhere on a Yorkshire road, one record rider accepted a small sweet from the other.</p><p><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1960-1969/`}>Read more about cycling in the 1960s.</a></p></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><p>The date, distances and record context have been checked across British cycling-history sources. The liquorice allsort story rests on Beryl Burton’s later account, her daughter Denise Burton-Cole’s retelling and Mike McNamara’s confirmation. Both photographs are freely licensed historical archive images; the exact year of the start photograph is not stated.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cycling/31641006">BBC Sport: Beryl Burton and the 12-hour record</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cyclingweekly.com/news/latest-news/beryl-burton-british-legend-62824">Cycling Weekly: Beryl Burton, British legend</a></li><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2019/aug/26/beryl-burton-liquorice-allsorts-shake-up-cycling">The Guardian: Burton, McNamara and the liquorice allsort</a></li><li><a href="https://www.yfanefa.com/record/6876">Yorkshire Film Archive: 12 Hour Time Trial Record</a></li><li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beryl_Burton_(14988376933).jpg">Wikimedia Commons: start photograph</a></li><li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beryl_Burton_1967.jpg">Wikimedia Commons: 1967 world championship photograph</a></li></ul></div></section>
